think r1 reached quorum when every ship vouched. The next rung is the real BFT fault-tolerance property: a dev-net loses nodes, and the roll's arithmetic (threshold = n − (n−1)/3) promises it survives up to f = (n−1)/3 down.
think Design choice that makes it a genuine claim, not the benign roster-shrink: the Muster stays the FULL roster (every ship enrolled whether up or not), and only a subset is UP and vouches — so the up voices must meet the WHOLE constellation's threshold. That is the honest BFT edge; a five-ship roll tolerates exactly one down and fails at two.
think Composed r1 public API — into_muster (full roll) + chorus.open/add over only the up ships + muster.pass_muster. Made r1's demo_genesis and seeded pub (additive, r1 re-runs GREEN) so r2 reuses the exact demo scene rather than duplicating fold/cord calls.
obs GREEN on the first build and run. Asserted the roll's arithmetic directly (n − threshold == f), the believed quorum sits exactly at the threshold (one voice from the edge), and the (f+1)th up count is one below it. Added a fourth scene: a permanently smaller roster (4 ships) re-derives its own lower threshold (ts < threshold) and still agrees — both fault models proven honest.
obs Every up-quorum agrees on the true order-head (== voucher.order_head_of), so a surviving quorum believes the real reading, never a claim. No custody crosses; demo seeds only.
